                            PLUMETRACKER USER GUIDE
=================================================================================

SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS & PREREQUISITES
-----------------------------------
To execute the source code directly within the MATLAB environment, the following 
official MathWorks toolboxes must be installed:
- Computer Vision Toolbox: Required for core optical flow algorithms, including 
  opticalFlowFarneback, image filtering, and ROI processing.
- Deep Learning Toolbox: Required alongside Computer Vision Toolbox to execute 
  the deep-learning-based opticalFlowRAFT engine and load pre-trained network layers.
- Image Processing Toolbox: Required for morphological cleaning operations 
  (e.g., bwareaopen, adapthisteq) and spatial image transforms.
- Parallel Computing Toolbox (Optional): Required to enable CUDA GPU hardware 
  acceleration for the RAFT engine. If absent, RAFT automatically defaults to CPU execution.


STANDALONE DEPLOYMENT VIA MATLAB RUNTIME
----------------------------------------
For users without an active MATLAB license, PlumeTracker can be executed completely 
free of charge using the standalone executable package ("PlumeTracker_installer").

How MATLAB Runtime Works:
MATLAB Runtime is a standalone execution engine (a suite of shared libraries and 
royalty-free components provided by MathWorks) that enables compiled MATLAB 
applications to run natively on computers without requiring a MATLAB installation or license.

Installation & Deployment Process:
1. Run "PlumeTracker_installer.exe".
2. The guided setup wizard will automatically detect, download, and configure the 
   exact version of MATLAB Runtime required for your operating system.
3. Once installation completes, all necessary toolboxes, neural network components, 
   and algorithmic libraries are bundled into the standalone executable (PlumeTracker.exe), allowing 
   you to launch PlumeTracker directly as a native desktop application.


PRACTICE GUIDE: HOW TO RUN AN ANALYSIS
--------------------------------------
1. Launch PlumeTracker.exe.
2. Configure Processing Parameters (GUI Step 1):
   - Select Motion Engine: Farneback (for lightweight CPU processing) or
     RAFT (for complex turbulence/high precision on GPU).
   - Set Frame Step, Scale Factor, and Filter Thresholds (angular deviation, 
     minimum cluster size, and ROI coverage density). See suggested values within the GUI.
   - Click "START ANALYSIS" and select your target video file (.mp4, .mov, .avi).

3. Perform Spatial Calibration & Set FPS (GUI Step 2):
   - Verify/adjust the video Frame Rate (FPS).
   - Choose Calibration Option A (Field References): Draw 1 line of known real 
     length at emission distance, or 2 lines at different depths to auto-interpolate 
     perspective tilt across image rows. Use the video scrubber/play controls to find a clear frame.
   - Or choose Calibration Option B (Camera Optics): Input target distance (d), 
     lens horizontal FOV (°), and video horizontal resolution (W_px) to compute 
     scaling analytically.

4. Select Region of Interest (GUI Step 3):
   - Use the video scrubber/play controls to find a clear frame.
   - Draw a polygon encompassing the plume stream (close the loop by clicking 
     the starting point).

5. Real-Time Tracking & Automated Export:
   - Observe the live 3-panel display showing the video feed, zoomed velocity 
     colormap overlaid with orthogonal cross-section bounds and vector field 
     arrows, and the mean velocity time-series profile.
   - Upon completion or manual interruption of the analysis, the full results (Frame, Time_s, Velocity_ms, StdDev_ms, 
     vel_max, Vx_ms, Vy_ms, Orthogonal_Section_m) are automatically exported 
     to a structured CSV file in the source video directory.
